// Auto-generated plain-text knowledge base from the Help Center articles.
// Used to ground Gemini's answers in the site's real content.
const KNOWLEDGE_BASE = `
### Getting started on Nexor Sparks (id: getting-started)
Welcome to Nexor Sparks. Follow the steps below to create a new account and set up your profile. Create an account: open the app or website and tap the Sign Up tab. Enter your email or phone number, a unique username, and a strong password. Confirm your email/phone with the verification code. Enter your age accurately — you must meet the minimum age policy to use Nexor Sparks. Complete your profile: add a clear profile photo and a short bio. Follow people based on your interests so your feed gets personalized. Adjust your notification and privacy settings to your liking. After logging in for the first time, we recommend reading the Privacy Policy and Community Guidelines at least once.

### Forgot your password? How to reset it (id: password-forgot)
If you've forgotten your password, don't worry — you can get back into your account in a few steps. Steps to reset your password: tap "Forgot Password?" on the login screen. Enter your registered email or phone number. You'll be sent a verification code or reset link — open it. Set a new, strong password (use a mix of letters, numbers, and symbols). Log in again with your new password. If you're not receiving the reset code: check your spam/junk folder. Confirm you entered the same email/number linked to your account. Wait a few minutes and try again. After resetting your password, if you think someone else may have had access to your account, be sure to read the Hacked Account guide.

### Changing your password while logged in (id: change-password)
If you already remember your current password but just want to update it, go to Settings > Change Password. Enter your current password to confirm it's really you, then enter and confirm your new password. Use a strong, unique password you don't reuse anywhere else. If you don't remember your current password, use the "Forgot Password?" flow from the login screen instead — see the Password Reset guide.

### Your account has been hacked or compromised (id: hacked-account)
If you think someone else has accessed your account, immediately follow the steps below. Signs of compromise: Pulses or messages are going out from your account that you didn't post. Your profile info (bio, photo, email, password) has changed on its own. You received a login/password change notification that you didn't make yourself. You're unable to log into your own account. What to do immediately: Change your password right away — if you can still log in, follow the "Password Reset" guide to set a new, strong, and unique password. If you can't log in — use the "Forgot Password" reset link to regain access. Check active sessions — go to Settings > Login Activity and check whether any unknown device is logged in, and log them out. Verify your connected email/phone — confirm the recovery email/number is really yours. Review recent activity — delete/undo any unauthorized Pulse, message, or profile change. If you can't reset your password because the attacker has also changed your recovery email/phone, submit an account recovery request via the AI Assistant (bottom right corner) or the "Report a Problem" page. Tips to keep your account safe in the future: turn on Two-Factor Login and Login Alerts in Settings, use a unique password everywhere, never enter your password on unfamiliar links or messages (avoid phishing), and keep checking your login activity regularly.

### Two-factor login and login alerts (id: two-factor-login)
Settings > Two-factor login adds an extra layer of security: once turned on, an email verification code is required every time you (or anyone else) tries to log into your account, in addition to your password. Settings > Login alerts sends you an email notification any time there's a new login to your account, so you can spot unauthorized access quickly. Settings > Login Activity lets you see every device/session currently logged into your account, so you can review and log out anything you don't recognize. Turning these three on together is the strongest way to protect your account.

### Linking a phone number (id: link-phone)
Adding a phone number to your account (Settings > Link phone number) gives you another way to recover your account if you ever lose access to your email, and can be used for verification codes. You'll receive an OTP (one-time code) by SMS to confirm the number really belongs to you before it's linked.

### Deactivating or deleting your account (id: delete-account)
You can either temporarily deactivate or permanently delete your account. Deactivate (temporary): when you deactivate, your profile, Pulses, and messages are all hidden, but no data is deleted. Everything becomes active again as soon as you log back in. Delete (permanent): permanently deleting removes your profile, Pulses, articles, messages, and data forever. This action cannot be undone, so save/download any important data first if needed. Go to the Account section in Settings. Choose the Deactivate or Delete Account option. Enter your password to confirm. If you just want a break, it's better to use Deactivate instead of Delete.

### Posting Pulses and Articles (id: pulses-articles)
On Nexor Sparks, you can share your moments as Pulses, or write longer content in the Articles format. Posting a Pulse: create your Pulse by adding a photo, text, or media. Be sure to preview it before posting. You can edit or delete it even after posting. Editing/deleting your content: go to your Pulse/Article and tap the (⋯) menu. Choose Edit or Delete. Once deleted, content cannot be restored. Articles unlock once your total views across your Pulses and Bolts reach a certain threshold shown on the locked-Articles screen — until then, keep posting Pulses/Bolts to grow your view count and the Articles tab will unlock automatically. Keep in mind while posting content: every Pulse and Article must follow our Community Guidelines. Content that violates the guidelines may be removed.

### Bolts — short-form videos/reels (id: bolts)
Bolts are Nexor Sparks' short-form vertical video feature (like Reels), found in the Bolts tab of the bottom navigation. Scroll through the full-screen Bolts feed to discover short videos from creators. You can like, comment, and share any Bolt. To send a Bolt to a friend, tap the paper-plane "Send to DM" icon on the Bolt — this opens the same Send-to-DM sheet used for sharing Pulses, letting you pick a conversation or friend to send it to directly, Instagram-style. Bolts are muted by default when the feed opens, just tap the video to unmute. Views on your own Bolts count toward unlocking the Articles feature.

### Stories (id: stories)
Stories are short-lived photo/video updates that appear at the top of the app and disappear after a set time, similar to Instagram Stories. Tap "Share to Story" to post one from a photo/video, or from an existing Pulse/Bolt via the share sheet. Story rings on profile photos show that a user has an active Story — tap it to view. You can reply directly to someone's Story using the reply box at the bottom of the Story viewer.

### Direct Messages (DMs) (id: direct-messages)
Nexor Sparks lets you message people privately via Direct Messages (DMs), accessible from the chat icon. You can send text, media, and GIFs in a conversation. You can also send a Pulse, Bolt, or profile directly into a DM conversation using the paper-plane "Send to DM" / share icon found on that content — pick one or more conversations or friends from the share sheet and it's sent instantly. Only people who can message you (based on your Privacy Settings) can start a new conversation with you.

### What is the "AI" label on AI-generated content (id: ai-content-label)
When content has been fully or significantly generated or edited with AI tools, you may see a golden "AI" label on it. Why this label matters: it keeps the community transparent about how content was made. It builds trust — people know whether content is a real photo/video or AI-generated. Labeling your own AI-generated content: if you're creating content with AI tools, label it honestly when posting. Posting misleading AI content without a label is considered a violation of the Community Guidelines.

### Verified badge and Business/Organization badge (id: verification)
Nexor Sparks has two visually distinct badges that appear next to a profile's name, and which one you see depends on the account type: Personal & Creator accounts that are verified show the standard blue verified tick. Business/Organization accounts that are verified show a separate business badge icon (instead of the plain blue tick) confirming the account belongs to an official organization or brand. Both badge types confirm the account's identity has been verified by our team — they don't imply any kind of endorsement or priority. Which badge you get is based on the Account Type set in Settings > Account Type (Personal, Creator, or Business/Organization) at the time you're verified. Beware of fake verified badges: if an account claims to be verified but doesn't have the real badge, report it via the "Report a Problem" page.

### How to get verified — identity verification form (id: get-verified)
To apply for a verified badge, go to Settings > Get Verified. This opens a full-page identity verification form, and every field is required: Legal name — your full legal name exactly as it appears on your ID. Username — your current @handle (shown automatically). Country — select the country that issued your ID. ID document type — Passport, National ID Card, or Driving License. ID photo — upload a clear, unedited photo of that document; your name and photo on it must be readable. Reason — explain in English, in at least 30 characters, who you are and why you should be verified. Links — provide 3 to 10 links (official social profiles and/or news articles) that help prove your identity, one per line. Finally you must tick the confirmation checkbox stating the information and ID are genuine and belong to you before submitting. This is a formal request that is reviewed manually by the team — it is not instant, and submitting false information can lead to rejection or account action. Which badge you receive (standard blue tick vs. the business badge) depends on your Account Type at the time of approval.

### Requesting a username change on a verified account (id: username-change-request)
Once your account is verified, you can no longer change your username directly from Edit Profile — this is intentional, to protect verified identities from being reused or impersonated. Instead, go to Settings and use "Request username change." Fill in the requested new username and your reason, then submit. Your request goes into manual review; you can only have one pending request at a time — check its status before submitting another. Non-verified accounts can still change their username directly and instantly from Edit Profile with no review needed.

### Account types: Personal, Creator, and Business/Organization (id: account-types)
Nexor Sparks supports three account types, changeable anytime from Settings > Account Type: Personal — the default account type for everyday users. Creator — for people building an audience, unlocks the Professional Dashboard with analytics. Business/Organization — for companies, brands, and official organizations, also unlocks the Professional Dashboard, and shows the distinct business badge instead of the plain blue tick if verified. Switching your account type automatically updates the badge style shown on all your existing Pulses, Bolts, Articles, and Stories retroactively, so your profile stays consistent everywhere.

### Professional dashboard / analytics (id: professional-dashboard)
Settings > Professional Dashboard gives Creator and Business accounts insights into their reach, likes, and views across their Pulses, Bolts, and Articles, including follower and following counts. This is a good place to track how your content is performing over time.

### Privacy settings, blocking, and safety tools (id: blocking-safety)
Nexor Sparks gives you several tools to control your experience. Private account (Settings > Private account): only your approved followers can see your Pulses. Blocking: when you block someone, they won't be able to see your profile, Pulses, or messages, and won't be able to contact you — open their profile and choose Block from the (⋯) menu, or manage all blocked accounts from Settings > Blocked accounts. Muting: when you mute someone (Settings > Muted accounts), they can still contact you, but you won't get notifications about their activity — and they won't know you've muted them. Blur sensitive content (Settings): automatically blurs detected adult/sensitive images until you tap to reveal them. Share location guess (Settings): controls whether an approximate "Account based in" country shows on your About info. If someone is harassing you, be sure to report them in addition to blocking them.

### Copyright strikes on your own account (id: copyright-strikes)
Settings > Copyright Strikes shows whether your account currently has any active copyright strikes issued after someone filed a valid copyright complaint against your content. Repeated copyright violations can lead to account suspension or a permanent ban — see the Copyright & Intellectual Property Policy for how the reporting/counter-request process works if you believe a strike was a mistake.

### Privacy Policy (id: privacy-policy)
This Privacy Policy explains how Nexor Sparks collects, uses, stores, and protects your information. What we collect: Account info: username, email/phone, password (encrypted), profile details. Content: your Pulses, articles, messages, comments. Usage data: how you use the app, device and log information. How we use it: to provide and personalize app services for you. For account safety and fraud prevention. To improve the app and fix bugs. Data sharing: we do not sell your personal data to third parties without consent. Data is only shared when legally required, or with service providers (like hosting) who work on our behalf. Your rights: the right to access, correct, or delete your data. The right to deactivate/delete your account at any time. Control who can see your content via privacy settings. Data retention: we retain data as long as your account is active. When you delete your account, data is deleted or anonymized in accordance with applicable law. For any privacy-related questions, talk to the AI Assistant or use the "Report a Problem" page.

### Terms of Service (id: terms-of-service)
By using Nexor Sparks, you agree to these Terms of Service. Eligibility: you must meet the minimum age requirement to use this app and must provide accurate information. Your responsibility: you are responsible for the security of your account and password. You are responsible for the accuracy and legality of the content you post. Creating multiple fake or impersonation accounts is not allowed. Content ownership: you remain the owner of the content you create. By posting, you grant Nexor Sparks only enough license to display and distribute the content on the app. Prohibited conduct: harassment, hate speech, spam, fraud, illegal content, and intellectual property violations are strictly prohibited — see Community Guidelines for details. Suspension and termination: repeated or serious violations of the Terms or Community Guidelines may result in account suspension or termination. Changes to Terms: we may update these Terms from time to time. Major changes will be communicated through the app.

### Community Guidelines (id: community-guidelines)
Nexor Sparks believes in building a safe, respectful, and authentic community. These guidelines apply to every user. Be authentic: use your real identity — impersonation is not allowed. Honestly label AI-generated content. Fake engagement (bots, bought followers) is not allowed. Be respectful: harassment, bullying, or threats are absolutely not allowed. Hate speech — based on race, religion, gender, sexuality, disability — is not allowed. Violating anyone's privacy (sharing personal info) is not allowed. Post safe content: graphic violence, gore, or disturbing content is not allowed. Nudity or sexually explicit content is not allowed. Content promoting self-harm is not allowed — if you're struggling, please talk to a trusted person or a local helpline right away. Promoting illegal activities (drugs, weapons, fraud) is not allowed. Spam and misinformation: repetitive spam, misleading links, and deliberately spreading false information are not allowed. What happens when you violate: content may be removed. A warning may be issued. Repeated or serious violations may result in temporary or permanent account suspension. If you see any content or user breaking the guidelines, report it right away via the "Report a Problem" page.

### Copyright & Intellectual Property Policy (id: copyright-policy)
Nexor Sparks respects others' intellectual property and expects the same from its users. Copyright rules: only post content you own, or that you have permission/license to post. Using others' photos, videos, music, or writing without permission is copyright infringement. If your copyrighted content has been used without permission: you can file a complaint through our dedicated copyright report page. You'll need to submit a link to the infringing content, proof of your ownership, and your contact details. File a Copyright Report. If your content was removed due to a copyright claim: if you believe the removal was a mistake (e.g. the content is your original work), you can submit a counter-request through the same report page. Accounts with repeated copyright violations may be suspended or permanently banned.

### Reporting content, an account, or a problem (id: report-content)
If you find any content, account, or behavior that goes against our Community Guidelines, it's important to report it. Reporting a Pulse/Article/Bolt: go to the post and tap the (⋯) menu. Select Report. Choose the most relevant reason (spam, harassment, nudity, hate speech, misinformation, etc.). Reporting an account: go to that user's profile. Choose Report Account from the (⋯) menu. State a reason and submit. Reporting an app bug or technical problem: describe the bug, crash, or technical issue to the AI Assistant (bottom right corner) along with a screenshot. Reports stay anonymous — the user you reported won't know who reported them. Emergency or immediate safety threat: if there is an immediate threat to someone's life or safety, contact your local emergency services first.

### About Nexor Sparks — company info (id: about-nexor)
Nexor Sparks is built and run by Nexor. Gursharan Singh is the Founder and CEO of Nexor. Anmol Sunda is the Co-Founder.
`;

// Cloudflare Pages Function — POST /api/chat
// Keeps the Gemini API key on the server. It is NEVER sent to the browser.
// Set GEMINI_API_KEY in: Cloudflare Pages dashboard → your project →
// Settings → Environment variables → Production (and Preview) → Add variable.

const SYSTEM_PROMPT = `You are "Sparky", the AI Help Assistant for Nexor Sparks, a social app where users post "Pulses" (short posts), "Bolts" (short-form videos), "Stories", "Articles", and message each other via DMs.
Answer briefly (2-5 sentences), in friendly, clear English, like Instagram's help bot.

You must answer ONLY using the information in the HELP CENTER KNOWLEDGE BASE below — this is the site's real, official help content.
Do not invent steps, policies, or features that are not written in the knowledge base.
If the knowledge base does not cover the user's question, say clearly that you don't have that information yet and suggest they use the "Report a Problem" page, instead of guessing.
Never claim to take real actions (like actually resetting a password) — only guide the user on the steps to do it themselves in the app, based on the knowledge base.

STAY ON TOPIC: this is a multi-turn conversation. Keep answering follow-up questions about the SAME topic the user is currently asking about (using the conversation history below for context) until the user clearly asks about something new. Don't jump to an unrelated topic on your own.

ALWAYS LINK: every reply must end with exactly one line in the exact format "LINK: <id>" using the (id: ...) of the single most relevant knowledge base article for this reply — this powers a real link shown to the user, so never skip it and never invent an id that isn't listed above.

===== HELP CENTER KNOWLEDGE BASE =====
${KNOWLEDGE_BASE}
===== END OF KNOWLEDGE BASE =====`;

export async function onRequestPost(context) {
  const { request, env } = context;

  if (!env.GEMINI_API_KEY) {
    return new Response(JSON.stringify({ error: "Server misconfigured: missing GEMINI_API_KEY" }), {
      status: 500,
      headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    });
  }

  let body;
  try {
    body = await request.json();
  } catch {
    return new Response(JSON.stringify({ error: "Invalid request body" }), {
      status: 400,
      headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    });
  }

  const userMessage = String(body.message || "").slice(0, 1500);
  if (!userMessage.trim()) {
    return new Response(JSON.stringify({ error: "Empty message" }), {
      status: 400,
      headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    });
  }

  // Optional, non-sensitive account context passed from the main app
  // (see openHelpCenter() in index.html). Only ever a display name,
  // handle, bio, verified flag, account type, and PUBLIC follower/
  // following/pulse counts — never an email, password, or any other
  // private data — so it's safe to fold into the prompt.
  let contextLine = "";
  const ctx = body.context;
  if (ctx && typeof ctx === "object") {
    const safeName = typeof ctx.name === "string" ? ctx.name.slice(0, 60) : null;
    const safeHandle = typeof ctx.handle === "string" ? ctx.handle.slice(0, 40) : null;
    const safeBio = typeof ctx.bio === "string" ? ctx.bio.slice(0, 300) : null;
    const bits = [];
    if (safeName) bits.push(`display name "${safeName}"`);
    if (safeHandle) bits.push(`handle "@${safeHandle}"`);
    if (ctx.verified) bits.push("a verified badge");
    if (ctx.accountType) bits.push(`account type "${ctx.accountType}"`);
    if (typeof ctx.followersCount === "number") bits.push(`${ctx.followersCount} followers`);
    if (typeof ctx.followingCount === "number") bits.push(`following ${ctx.followingCount} accounts`);
    if (typeof ctx.pulsesCount === "number") bits.push(`${ctx.pulsesCount} Pulses posted`);
    if (safeBio) bits.push(`bio "${safeBio}"`);
    if (bits.length) {
      contextLine = `\n\nThe user asking is currently logged in with ${bits.join(", ")}. You may greet them by name and reference these details naturally when relevant (e.g. suggesting they check their own follower count, or personalizing tone based on account type), but do not claim to know anything else about their account beyond what's listed here.`;
    }
  }

  // Optional prior conversation turns, sent by the client, so the model
  // keeps answering about the same topic across follow-up messages
  // instead of losing context on every request. Each item is
  // { role: 'user' | 'model', text: string }. Capped defensively.
  const history = Array.isArray(body.history) ? body.history.slice(-12) : [];
  const contents = [];
  for (const turn of history) {
    if (!turn || typeof turn !== "object") continue;
    const role = turn.role === "model" ? "model" : "user";
    const text = String(turn.text || "").slice(0, 1500);
    if (!text.trim()) continue;
    contents.push({ role, parts: [{ text }] });
  }
  contents.push({
    role: "user",
    parts: [{ text: `${SYSTEM_PROMPT}${contextLine}\n\nUser message: ${userMessage}` }],
  });

  try {
    const resp = await fetch(
      "https://generativelanguage.googleapis.com/v1beta/models/gemini-flash-latest:generateContent",
      {
        method: "POST",
        headers: {
          "Content-Type": "application/json",
          "X-goog-api-key": env.GEMINI_API_KEY,
        },
        body: JSON.stringify({ contents }),
      }
    );

    if (!resp.ok) {
      const errText = await resp.text();
      return new Response(JSON.stringify({ error: "AI service error", detail: errText }), {
        status: 502,
        headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
      });
    }

    const data = await resp.json();
    let reply =
      data?.candidates?.[0]?.content?.parts?.[0]?.text?.trim() ||
      "Sorry, I wasn't able to generate a reply right now. Please try rephrasing your question.";

    // Pull out the "LINK: <id>" line the model was instructed to append,
    // so the client can render a real, accurate article link/button
    // instead of guessing one from keywords.
    let link = null;
    const linkMatch = reply.match(/\n?LINK:\s*([a-z0-9-]+)\s*$/i);
    if (linkMatch) {
      link = linkMatch[1].toLowerCase();
      reply = reply.slice(0, linkMatch.index).trim();
    }

    return new Response(JSON.stringify({ reply, link }), {
      headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    });
  } catch (err) {
    return new Response(JSON.stringify({ error: "Request failed" }), {
      status: 500,
      headers: { "Content-Type": "application/json" },
    });
  }
}
